<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edict of Gülhane</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1839">1839</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Gülhane Hatt-ı Şerifi, Supreme Edict of Gülhane, Tanzimât Fermânı, Imperial Edict of Reorganization, Rose Garden Edict</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Edict of Gülhane (Gülhane Hatt-ı Şerifi), also called the Tanzimât Fermânı, was a proclamation by the Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Abdülmecid I in 1839 that launched the Tanzimât period of reform in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. Issued at the behest of the reformist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-vizier">Grand Vizier</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-resid-pasha">Mustafa Reşid Pasha</a> and named after Gülhane Park, where it was first proclaimed, it promised security of life, honour and property, public trials, a regular system of taxation in place of tax farming and reformed conscription, to all subjects whatever their religion. It made royal promises rather than legal changes and was never fully implemented; after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-war">Crimean War</a> it was followed by the Imperial Reform Edict of 1856.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edict-of-gulhane.jpg" alt="Edict of Gülhane" width="330" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The Graphic · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ottoman_Parliament_1877.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Edict of Gülhane</b> promised every subject security of life, property and honour, fair taxation and conscription, and public trials; the sultan said he wished “to bring the benefits of a good administration to the provinces of the Ottoman Empire through new institutions”.</blockquote><a href="/movement/tanzimat">Tanzimat · Aims</a></li></ul></section>
